History shows that unethical methods often lead to unintended negative consequences that outweigh any positive outcomes.
Unethical means can corrupt the purpose of the end, making it lose its moral significance.
The integrity of the end is compromised when the means are morally questionable, undermining the overall value of the goal.
If the path to achieving an end is tainted by immorality, the end itself may become tainted, losing its original value.
The value of an end is not just in its outcome, but in the ethical journey toward it.